  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "Full paper text is present. Air-Bench results differ across tables and in the prose; the appendix also includes noisy extracted figure text.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The study evaluates a decoding-layer change on a safety benchmark, a different intervention and measure from fine-tuning or refusal-rate tests.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports Air-Bench scores under Confident Decoding and other decoding methods, so those results bear on whether a decoding change compromises safety. It does not report harmful-request refusal rates, prefilling, adversarial suffixes, or aligned-versus-base per-token KL on harmful prompts. Its layer-wise entropy and residual-stream analyses are on reasoning prompts and are not results for V3. Discussion of prior safety work is not counted as a result.",
    "test_reason": "The paper proposes Confident Decoding, which reads logits from a near-final 'entropy valley' layer to improve reasoning. Air-Bench appears only as a check that the method keeps safety. The decoding change is not an attack and does not target the response opening. The paper reports no prefilling, suffix or adversarial-decoding results. It reports no first-token or per-position KL between aligned and base models, and no harmful fine-tuning. Qi et al. (2406.05946) is cited only in related work, to say alignment effects concentrate in upper layers. So these results do not bear on whether shallow alignment lets openings or decoding exploits bypass refusal. The Air-Bench numbers belong under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Largely correct, but the main conclusion concerns reasoning. The authors argue final layers inject an alignment perturbation (an 'alignment tax') that harms reasoning. Bypassing it via entropy-valley layer selection gives gains on GPQA-D, HLE, Omni-MATH and LCB at under 2% latency. Safety is a secondary claim: Air-Bench stays robust or improves, and they note a −3.0 drop for Qwen3.5-27B.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "Inference-time selection of logits from a near-final entropy valley rather than fine-tuning or testing response-token KL; no safety training or prefilling intervention.",
    "method_sensitivity": "Air-Bench scores vary by model and reported table. The paper also reports conflicting Air-Bench values for some models across Tables 1, 2, 9, and 10.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Reading logits from a near-final entropy-valley layer (about 2–3% of tokens changed) leaves Air-Bench-2024 safety scores roughly flat or slightly higher in most instruct models (e.g. gpt-oss-120B 88→90). Qwen3.5-27B drops 1–3 points. Scoring uses a gpt-5.4 judge, 5 samples per leaf category.",
        "proposed_claim": "Non-adversarial inference-time layer selection near the final layer does not degrade safety-benchmark scores in most aligned models.",
